---
// Plain answers to the questions of a new visitor, in the order of their
// decision, then the practical questions (#333). Every answer is two to four
// sentences without a command, a flag or a file name, then one link to the
// page that holds the detail: the commands, the approval rules and the
// baseline stay in the docs. The facts come from the repository and the docs
// (configuration-and-security, adopting, troubleshooting, getting-started,
// risk-policy); "unattended" is described as Features and Configuration and
// security describe it (#328, consistency rule 4).
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import PageHead from "../components/PageHead.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import { href, issue } from "../lib/links";

const questions = [
  { id: "what", title: "What does Agent Codeyard do?" },
  { id: "replace", title: "Why use it next to Claude Code or Codex?" },
  { id: "who", title: "Do I need to be able to program?" },
  { id: "software", title: "What software can I build or maintain with it?" },
  { id: "existing", title: "Can I use an existing project?" },
  { id: "permission", title: "What do I still do myself?" },
  { id: "cost", title: "What does it cost, and why does that differ per project?" },
  { id: "too-heavy", title: "When is this way of working worth it?" },
  { id: "checked", title: "How do I know what was checked?" },
  { id: "wrong", title: "What happens when the AI gets stuck or does something wrong?" },
  { id: "data", title: "Where does it run, and which data goes to the AI providers?" },
  { id: "online", title: "Is the software also put online automatically?" },
  { id: "environments", title: "Which computers does it run on?" },
  { id: "stop", title: "How do I stop a run, and continue it?" },
  { id: "unattended", title: "What does an unattended run do without me?" },
  { id: "review", title: "Is every change reviewed?" },
];
const title = (id: string) => questions.find((question) => question.id === id)?.title ?? id;
---
<Base title="FAQ" description="Plain answers about Agent Codeyard: what it does, why next to Claude Code or Codex, whether you need to program, existing projects, what you still do, cost, when it is worth it, checks, mistakes, data and deployment.">
  <PageHead title="FAQ" lede="Plain answers to the first questions about Agent Codeyard, each with a link to the detail.">
    <ol class="contents">
      {questions.map((question) => <li><a href={`#${question.id}`}>{question.title}</a></li>)}
    </ol>
  </PageHead>

  <Section id="what" title={title("what")}>
    <p>AI coding tools such as Claude Code and Codex can write software code from a description. Agent Codeyard gives their work a fixed route: clarify what you need, plan the work, then build and check each change. You review the result and decide whether it becomes part of your project.</p>
    <p class="needs">The route step by step: <a href={href("workflow/")}>How it works</a>.</p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="replace" title={title("replace")}>
    <p>Claude Code and Codex can plan, ask questions, run tests and review on their own. Codeyard turns that into one process: written agreements, fixed roles, checks, and progress the next session can pick up. It writes no code itself; the coding tools do, and you keep your editor.</p>
    <p class="needs">What the process adds: <a href={href("features/")}>Features</a>.</p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="who" title={title("who")}>
    <p>To understand what it does, no. To use it, you work in a terminal with Git and GitHub, and it is not a no-code tool. You also need to judge the questions the AI asks and the change it proposes.</p>
    <p class="needs">The basic ideas first: <a href={href("docs/new-to-agentic-coding/")}>New to agentic coding?</a></p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="software" title={title("software")}>
    <p>Software that lives in a Git repository on GitHub and that you will keep extending, such as an application, a library or a course. Codeyard runs your project's own checks, so it is not tied to one language. Illustrative example: a gym's member application, where trainers record attendance for their classes and members see their own training history.</p>
    <p class="needs">Real projects and their records: <a href={href("examples/")}>Projects</a>.</p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="existing" title={title("existing")}>
    <p>Yes. Codeyard first reads your project and reports what it would add, without changing anything. You then review the addition like any other change, and it records which checks already fail, so old failures stay visible as known. Besides the template's own tests on example repositories, one pilot on a private, existing application ran on 6 November 2026; <a href={issue(170)}>its report</a> describes it in general terms.</p>
    <p class="needs">All steps: <a href={href("docs/adopting/")}>Adopting Agent Codeyard in an existing repository</a>.</p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="permission" title={title("permission")}>
    <p>You answer the questions and approve the requirements and the plan before anything is built. You answer when an agent stops with a question, and you read each proposed change and decide whether to merge it; no script merges. Whether GitHub itself refuses a merge depends on your repository settings and the rights of your account.</p>
    <p class="needs">Which changes always need you: <a href={href("docs/configuration-and-security/#gate")}>Changes that need the owner</a>.</p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="cost" title={title("cost")}>
    <p>Codeyard itself is free and open source, under the MIT licence. You pay for the AI models through your own Claude Code and Codex accounts, by subscription or paid API use, with their usage limits. The amount depends on the size of a change, the number of review rounds and the models your settings choose. That is why no price per feature can be given.</p>
    <p class="needs">What a few recorded runs took: <a href={href("docs/engineering-records/#tradeoff")}>More rounds find more, and cost more</a>.</p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="too-heavy" title={title("too-heavy")}>
    <p>Questions, a plan, checks and a second review cost time and model use for every change. That pays off for software you will extend and maintain, where a wrong assumption costs more later. For a quick experiment, a coding tool on its own is faster. A project can also ask for less process on low-risk changes.</p>
    <p class="needs">How much checking each kind of change gets: <a href={href("docs/risk-policy/")}>Risk policy</a>.</p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="checked" title={title("checked")}>
    <p>Every proposed change says which of your project's checks ran, what the review found and what was decided about each finding. The AI that built it also states, per agreed requirement, what shows that the requirement is met. A passed check is evidence of what that check examines, not proof that nothing else is wrong.</p>
    <p class="needs">What the evidence holds: <a href={href("docs/verification-and-completion/")}>Verification and completion</a>.</p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="wrong" title={title("wrong")}>
    <p>When an agent needs a decision only you can make, it stops and asks instead of guessing. A failing check is repaired and run again a limited number of times; after that, the run stops and says why. A review finding is fixed and the fix is reviewed again, and nothing becomes part of your project until you merge it.</p>
    <p class="needs">What each stop means and what to do: <a href={href("docs/troubleshooting/")}>Troubleshooting</a>.</p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="data" title={title("data")}>
    <p>Codeyard runs on your own machine or server; no Codeyard service sits in between. The coding tools send what they read and write, such as your code and answers, to their providers: Anthropic for Claude Code, OpenAI for Codex. Those providers' terms apply to that data. The records of each run stay on your machine, and your requirements, Issues and proposed changes are in your repository on GitHub.</p>
    <p class="needs">What an agent may do on your machine: <a href={href("docs/configuration-and-security/")}>Configuration and security</a>.</p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="online" title={title("online")}>
    <p>No. Codeyard ends with a proposed change, and the merge is yours. It sets up no deployment and leaves your existing automation as it is. If your project publishes itself after a merge, that is your own setup at work.</p>
    <p class="needs">What an adoption leaves alone: <a href={href("docs/adopting/#what-is-preserved")}>What is preserved</a>.</p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="environments" title={title("environments")}>
    <p>It runs in a Bash shell on Linux, with Git, the GitHub command-line tool, Claude Code and Codex. Parts of it also work on macOS, but no automated test covers macOS, and Windows is not supported. A check that ships with it tells you what is missing.</p>
    <p class="needs">What to install: <a href={href("docs/getting-started/")}>Getting started</a>.</p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="stop" title={title("stop")}>
    <p>Stop a run like any command in the terminal. Nothing is merged, and the work stays in its own working folder. Run the same command again and it continues where the feature stands, skipping the steps that are done. If it stopped for a decision of yours, answer first in the handoff note or on the Issue.</p>
    <p class="needs">The details: <a href={href("docs/troubleshooting/#a-session-or-a-run-was-interrupted")}>A session or a run was interrupted</a>.</p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="unattended" title={title("unattended")}>
    <p>An unattended run takes a feature from its Issue to a proposed change, between your decisions. Its writing agent asks you nothing: what it would have asked you is decided by the tool's own sandbox or permission check, and whatever would still need your answer is refused. The run approves the decision an agent proposes for each review finding, and records it. It stops when it needs your decision or reaches a limit or a failure, and it never merges.</p>
    <p class="needs">How each agent handles what it would have asked: <a href={href("docs/configuration-and-security/#unattended")}>What "unattended" means</a>.</p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="review" title={title("review")}>
    <p>Under the shipped settings, yes: an AI of the other provider reads every change and can only read. A project can let low-risk changes, such as documentation, skip the review; such a change says so and asks for your approval of the merge. A high-risk change is always reviewed.</p>
    <p class="needs">When the review is skipped: <a href={href("docs/risk-policy/")}>Risk policy</a>.</p>
  </Section>
</Base>
